From: gstreamer-custom-build local patches
Subject: [PATCH] mpegvideoparse: interpolate the PTS of frames that have none

mpegvideoparse asks GstBaseParse not to interpolate, so a frame whose PES
packet carried no PTS reaches the decoder with GST_CLOCK_TIME_NONE rather than
with the previous timestamp advanced by one frame duration. 0001 makes the
same change to h264parse.

**Why this tree wants it is not recorded, and this header does not invent a
reason.** The change arrived with no description and no measurement; what is
written above is a reading of the diff, not an account of the problem it was
for. It is one of two patches in this directory - 0001 is the other - whose
justification cannot be checked against anything, so treat it as load-bearing
until somebody establishes otherwise rather than as something safe to drop.

Split out on 2026-09-16. The patch file this came from also carried an
unrelated hunk against gst/mpegdemux/gstmpegdemux.c, modifying the base_time
adjustment that 0010 later deleted outright; 0010 had therefore been written
against this tree's modified copy of that block rather than against upstream.
That hunk has been removed and 0010 regenerated. The built source is unchanged
by the split.

--- a/_p2_/gst/videoparsers/gstmpegvideoparse.c
+++ b/_p2_/gst/videoparsers/gstmpegvideoparse.c
@@ -177,7 +177,7 @@
 {
   parse->config_flags = FLAG_NONE;
 
-  gst_base_parse_set_pts_interpolation (GST_BASE_PARSE (parse), FALSE);
+  gst_base_parse_set_pts_interpolation (GST_BASE_PARSE (parse), TRUE);
   gst_base_parse_set_infer_ts (GST_BASE_PARSE (parse), FALSE);
   GST_PAD_SET_ACCEPT_INTERSECT (GST_BASE_PARSE_SINK_PAD (parse));
   GST_PAD_SET_ACCEPT_TEMPLATE (GST_BASE_PARSE_SINK_PAD (parse));
